TITLE:: DSPAlgorithms
summary:: Enhanced Digital Signal Processing algorithms for auditory scene analysis.
categories:: DSP, Analysis
related:: Classes/GammatoneFilter, Classes/YinEstimator, Classes/CqtEstimator, Classes/FormantDetector

DESCRIPTION::
DSPAlgorithms is a comprehensive library of specialized signal processing tools for the Listening project. It contains SuperCollider ports of high-performance algorithms originally written in Rust and C++, specifically tailored for auditory modeling and pitch estimation.

The library provides:
LIST::
## STRONG::GammatoneFilter::: A 4th-order complex gammatone filter, simulating the frequency response of the human cochlea.
## STRONG::GammatoneFilterbank::: A collection of gammatone filters spaced according to the ERB (Equivalent Rectangular Bandwidth) scale.
## STRONG::YinEstimator::: An implementation of the YIN pitch estimation algorithm, featuring absolute thresholding and parabolic interpolation.
## STRONG::CqtEstimator::: A Constant-Q Transform implementation for logarithmic frequency analysis.
## STRONG::FormantDetector::: LPC-based (Linear Predictive Coding) vocal formant detection.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

EXAMPLES::

code::
// Using the GammatoneFilterbank
b = GammatoneFilterbank.new(60, 4000, 32);
s = SinOsc.ar(440);
b.processFrame(s.next);

// Using the YinEstimator
y = YinEstimator.new;
f = FloatArray.fill(2048, { |i| (i * 440 * 2 * pi / 44100).sin });
p = y.estimate(f);
postf("Detected Pitch: % Hz (Confidence: %)\n", p[0], p[1]);
::

THEORETICAL BACKGROUND::
The algorithms in this class are designed to bridge the gap between "machine listening" and "human listening." 

The link::Classes/GammatoneFilter::, for instance, uses a cascade of four first-order complex filters to approximate the impulse response of the auditory nerve. The impulse response is given by:
formula::
g(t) = a \cdot t^{n-1} \cdot e^{-2\pi bt} \cdot \cos(2\pi f_c t + \phi)
::
Where n=4 is the order, and b is the bandwidth related to the ERB.

The link::Classes/YinEstimator:: improves upon standard autocorrelation by using the Cumulative Mean Normalized Difference Function (CMNDF), which significantly reduces "octave errors" common in pitch detection.